From d325832e349ac4d4ae0b47f1612ad31674bff9b2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 26 Jul 2026 13:40:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Fix truncated slideshow renders and stop retrying a missing
 video API token
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A photo slideshow with a background music track shorter than the video came
out as long as the track and no longer: the music was a plain unlooped input
and the encode carried -shortest, so a one-second bed under a five-second
slideshow produced a one-second file. That is an 80% duration drift, well past
the post-render QA gate's 25% tolerance, so the job then failed and refunded
after paying for the whole render. The bed is now looped to cover the video and
-shortest is gone, leaving the existing -t output bound to set the length. The
loop count is counted rather than infinite, because -stream_loop -1 on an input
that opens but decodes to no packets — a truncated upload, and musicPath is
validated by object-key prefix only — restarts the demuxer forever without ever
producing the frame that -t would bound, hanging the encode until the
five-minute ffmpeg timeout. A bed whose length ffprobe cannot read is not
looped at all, and one already longer than the video needs no loop; since the
intro-skip analyser only returns a nonzero seek for a track longer than the
video, a seek and a loop can no longer coincide.

Every still fed to ffmpeg ran at the image demuxer's 25fps default while the
filter chain retimed to 30, so both the slideshow and the topic video's Ken
Burns b-roll clips came out at 25/30ths of their intended length and the
zoompan move, stepped once per input frame, under-travelled by the same
fraction. Slideshows lost about 17% per slide — a 6.7% overall drift that sits
inside the QA gate and shipped short in silence — and for slide durations above
three seconds the xfade offsets overran the shortened streams badly enough to
lose more than half the video. AI b-roll clips came back short enough that the
composer loop-filled them with -stream_loop -1, which is what made the camera
move visibly restart mid-scene. Both inputs are now pinned with -framerate to
the same FPS constant their filter chains already use.

VideoGenNotConfiguredError extends Error rather than VideoGenProviderError, so
it fell through isTransientVideoGenError's closing `return error instanceof
Error` and was classified as an upstream hiccup. A tenant with no Replicate
token therefore walked all three models in the fallback chain — none of which
could have worked, since they authenticate with the same credential resolved
once outside the loop — and recorded three failures against the shared provider
circuit breaker, which is enough to open it and turn later preflights into a
misleading 503 for tenants who are configured. Classifying it as terminal
delivers both halves at once: the fallback loop rethrows a non-transient
primary failure before it reaches the recording call, and only records failures
it classified as transient.

The argv construction for both renders is now a pure exported function
(buildSlideshowArgs, buildStillToClipArgs) so input-option ordering is
assertable without spawning an encoder; the filtergraphs are unchanged.

